# Line endings are decided here, once, rather than in each developer's checkout.
#
# The symptom this fixes: `core.autocrlf=true` with no `.gitattributes`, on Windows.
# Git checked files out as CRLF, Prettier wanted LF, and `npm run verify` failed on
# 35 files that nobody had edited - including README.md, firebase.json and files
# untouched for months. Every fix was the same non-fix: run `prettier --write`, which
# writes LF, which git converts back to CRLF on the next checkout. A gate that fails
# for a reason unrelated to your change is a gate people learn to ignore, or to
# `--force` past.
#
# `eol=lf` is the important half. With only `text=auto`, git still applies the local
# autocrlf on checkout; `eol=lf` pins the working tree to LF regardless of the
# developer's OS, which is also what the containerised build and CI see.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect binary content rather than being told, so the SVG icons
# stay text (and stay diffable) without a rule per asset.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Genuinely binary. Marked `-text` so git neither scans nor converts it.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary

# package-lock.json is deliberately NOT listed. npm writes it as text on every
# platform, so it wants the same normalisation as everything else - marking it
# `-text` would exempt it and let whatever a single machine emitted become the
# committed truth.